{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>I am too small for all the kindnesses and all the truth</b> — my merits have diminished through the kindnesses and truth that You have done with me; therefore I fear lest from the time You promised me I have become corrupted through sin and it will cause me to be delivered into the hand of Esau. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 32:11). But it is not correct in the language of Scripture. Moreover, he said afterward, \"And You said, I will surely do good with you, and I will make your seed as the sand of the sea\" — and what would that promise avail if sin had caused it afterward? Furthermore, Jacob mentioned two promises that the Holy One, blessed be He, had promised him at Beth El and in Haran, and he said first what was said to him in Haran: \"YHWH who said to me, Return to your land and to your birthplace, and I will do good with you\" — that is what was said to him when he left Laban (Genesis 31:3): \"Return to the land of your fathers and to your birthplace, and I will be with you.\" Now, after that, He did not do good to him through all these kindnesses and all this truth, such that his merits would diminish on their account. Rather, \"I am too small\" means that he is too small to be worthy of all the kindnesses that He has done with him, as in \"Who shall rise, Jacob, for he is small\" (Amos 7:2) — from being able to bear all that is decreed upon him. So too they said (Genesis Rabbah 76:5): \"I am too small\" — Rabbi Abba said: I am not worthy. And the kindnesses are the benefits He did with him without an oath, and the truth is the good that He promised and fulfilled His promise to him. He says that he is not worthy that He should promise him and do for him those good things that He promised, nor the many other good things He did with him. And I have not understood the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 32:11), who translated \"from all kindnesses and from all good,\" whereas he is accustomed to translate חסד ואמת as \"good and truth\" (above 24:49, below 47:29). Perhaps the doing of kindnesses refers to the deliverance that He delivered him many times from all his troubles, and the doing of truth refers to all this good that he had, for He gave him sons and daughters, wealth and property, and honor. But what seems correct in my eyes is that kindnesses that are enduring are called truth, for it is from the root אמנה, in the sense of \"and your house and your kingdom shall be established (ונאמן) forever\" (II Samuel 7:16) — the meaning being permanence; \"its bread is given, its waters are faithful (נאמנים)\" (Isaiah 33:16), as he said: \"Will you be to me like a deceitful brook, waters that do not endure (לא נאמנו)\" (Jeremiah 15:18)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The verse citations embedded within Ramban's text (Genesis 24:49, 47:29, II Samuel 7:16, Isaiah 33:16, Jeremiah 15:18) appear as parenthetical Hebrew shorthand in the original; I have rendered them in standardized form while preserving his argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:41.111595118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}